FrameCryptor: AES-256-GCM when the shared key material is 32 bytes.

The AEAD already selects AES-256-GCM when the derived key is 32 bytes
(GetAesGcmAlgorithmFromKeySize). ParticipantKeyHandler::SetKeyFromMaterial
hardcoded the derived length to 128 bits; derive it from the key-material size
instead (32 -> 256, else 128). No Java/JNI API change — the app picks the cipher
by the byte length it passes to setKey()/setSharedKey(). ratchet_window_size=0
so the .cc ratchet paths are unused. Ref: livekit/client-sdk-android#952.

W-AES256DIAG (temporary, this build only): also logs pw_len/pw_hex/derived_len/
derived_hex via __android_log_print(ANDROID_LOG_ERROR, "AES256DIAG", ...) right
after every SetKeyFromMaterial call, Android-only (#if WEBRTC_ANDROID), to
verify at runtime that a 32-byte password really derives a 32-byte key on the
actual linked native library — cross-platform group-call E2EE interop bug,
Desktop<->Android decrypt fails both directions despite byte-identical SK_0 at
every application layer. Remove once verified.

diff --git a/api/crypto/frame_crypto_transformer.h b/api/crypto/frame_crypto_transformer.h
